{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>For.</b> I have done — [a word denoting] strength:" "<b>I have understanding.</b> From the two [letters] visible in the niphal paradigm." "<b>And I removed.</b> Future [tense], but the sense is [historical] past, like יעשו עגל בחורב (Psalms 106:19)." "<b>And their fortified cities I have destroyed.</b> From the root of ועתדה בשדה לך (Proverbs 24:26) — the money a person prepares to have available for future days." "<b>And I brought down like a mighty one.</b> If it has an alef, it is plene [the alef is added], and the sense is: I brought down those who sit in the palace כביר [like a mighty one]. And Rabbi Moses said that the alef is a root letter, like אביר הרועים (1 Samuel 21:8), and the sense is: like a mighty one."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "Resolving זמן עומד literally as 'standing time'; Ibn Ezra's term for past tense rendered as '[historical] past' to convey the syntactic point without over-translating.", 4 "Variant reading: the first clause assumes כאביר with intrusive alef, while Rabbi Moses (Ibn Ezra's contemporary) reads the alef as root, comparing אביר 'mighty one'; the second reference to כביר in Ibn Ezra's own gloss appears to cite the word without the alef, matching his view."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:53:16.142773668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}